<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Trim-warning suppressions for third-party assemblies that we cannot modify.
  Deliberately MINIMAL — this is not a copy of ClaudeForge's file. Every entry
  below was observed in an actual trimmed publish of THIS app (Release, the
  linux-x64 RID, self-contained, with trimming on).

  Measured 2026-09-03: after the McpServersAccessor IL2026 was fixed at its call
  site, DataGrid was the only remaining trim complaint in the whole closure.
  Re-measured 2026-09-11, when the Backup / Restore page introduced this app's
  first real DataGrid: still the only complaint, but two more codes — see the
  block below, whose earlier safety argument that comment lapsed on the spot.

  What is NOT here, and why (verified by listing the transitive package closure
  with `dotnet list package`, not assumed):

    * Markdown.Avalonia / ColorTextBlock.Avalonia / Svg.Model / Svg.Custom /
      Svg.Controls.Skia.Avalonia — ClaudeForge suppresses and roots all five.
      NONE of them is in OpenCodeForge's package closure. OpenCodeForge therefore
      carries no <TrimmerRootAssembly> entries at all; adding them would name
      assemblies that do not exist in this app.

    * Semi.Avalonia — IS in the closure (via LayeredEditors.Avalonia's
      SemiBundle.axaml) but emits no warnings here. ClaudeForge's Semi.Avalonia
      suppressions exist because Svg.Skia widens ILLink's reachability graph into
      Semi's compiled AXAML; OpenCodeForge has no Svg.Skia, so that graph is
      never widened. Do not add a Semi entry pre-emptively — if one is ever
      needed it will announce itself as a real warning.

    * ExCSS / Serilog — no observed warnings in this app's closure.

  Wiring: referenced from OpenCodeForge.csproj via
  `<_ILLinkSuppressions Include="ILLink.Suppressions.xml"/>`. The leading
  underscore is load-bearing — `<LinkAttributesXml>`, `<TrimmerRootDescriptor>`
  and an `ILLink.LinkAttributes.xml` EmbeddedResource all FAIL SILENTLY or emit
  spurious IL2007/IL2101. The long-form explanation of all three dead ends lives
  in ClaudeForge.csproj (see the comment above its own _ILLinkSuppressions
  ItemGroup) and in src/ClaudeForge/ILLink.Suppressions.xml; it is not repeated
  here so the two files cannot drift into disagreeing.
-->
<linker>

    <!--
      Avalonia.Controls.DataGrid — reflection-based column, converter and
      collection-view code paths that upstream has never annotated.

      ⛔ THE "NO DATAGRID ANYWHERE" JUSTIFICATION HAS LAPSED, exactly as the
      previous version of this comment said it would. As of the Backup / Restore
      page (Views/BackupRestoreView.axaml) this app hosts a real DataGrid: the
      list of available backup archives. The assembly is no longer merely in the
      closure via LayeredEditors.Avalonia's SemiBundle theme — its code runs.

      So ClaudeForge's reasoning applies here now, and it is a claim about the
      MARKUP rather than about reachability:

        * Every column is a DataGridTemplateColumn whose CellTemplate sets
          x:DataType="vm:BackupRowViewModel", so each cell binding is COMPILED.
          Nothing resolves a cell's value through DefaultValueConverter or a
          string-typed Binding, which is what IL2026 is about.
        * AutoGenerateColumns="False". GenerateColumn — the IL2026 site — is
          reachable only from auto-generation, which this grid never asks for.
        * ⚠ SortMemberPath IS a string resolved by reflection (IL2075, via
          DataGridDataConnection.UpdateDataProperties and
          DataGridCollectionView.GetItemType). Every path it names —
          DisplayDate, DisplayName, DisplayPlatform, DisplayMode,
          DisplayClients, DisplaySize — is ALSO bound by a compiled binding in
          the same column's template, so ILLink roots each one and the lookup
          finds it. ⛔ A future column that sorts on a property no template
          binds would trim to a silently unsortable column; add the property to
          a template, or drop its SortMemberPath.

      Codes below are the five actually observed in a trimmed Release publish of
      THIS app (win-x64, self-contained), not a copy of ClaudeForge's list:
      IL2026 and IL2075 arrived with the DataGrid above; IL2070 predates it.
      IL2104 is listed alongside them so the suppression holds whether or not
      TrimmerSingleWarn is on.
    -->
    <assembly fullname="Avalonia.Controls.DataGrid">
        <attribute fullname="System.Diagnostics.CodeAnalysis.UnconditionalSuppressMessageAttribute">
            <argument>Trimming</argument>
            <argument>IL2026</argument>
            <property name="Scope">module</property>
            <property name="Target">Avalonia.Controls.DataGrid</property>
        </attribute>
        <attribute fullname="System.Diagnostics.CodeAnalysis.UnconditionalSuppressMessageAttribute">
            <argument>Trimming</argument>
            <argument>IL2070</argument>
            <property name="Scope">module</property>
            <property name="Target">Avalonia.Controls.DataGrid</property>
        </attribute>
        <attribute fullname="System.Diagnostics.CodeAnalysis.UnconditionalSuppressMessageAttribute">
            <argument>Trimming</argument>
            <argument>IL2075</argument>
            <property name="Scope">module</property>
            <property name="Target">Avalonia.Controls.DataGrid</property>
        </attribute>
        <attribute fullname="System.Diagnostics.CodeAnalysis.UnconditionalSuppressMessageAttribute">
            <argument>Trimming</argument>
            <argument>IL2104</argument>
            <property name="Scope">module</property>
            <property name="Target">Avalonia.Controls.DataGrid</property>
        </attribute>
    </assembly>

</linker>
